{
  "unit": {
    "id": "8",
    "title": "Counting and time",
    "blurb": "One, eleven to twenty, and telling the hour",
    "intro": "_ūnus, ūna, ūnum_ (one) is declined like an adjective: _ūnus puer_, _ūna puella_. The numbers eleven to twenty never change, like four to ten: _ūndecim, duodecim, trēdecim_ and on up to _vīgintī_ (twenty). To ask the time, say _Quota hōra est?_ (literally “what number hour is it?”) and answer with an ordinal number, which agrees with _hōra_: _Hōra tertia est_ (it is the third hour). To say AT an hour, put _hōra_ and the ordinal in the ablative: _hōrā tertiā_."
  },
